<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-col-row-null-space-compute-three-spaces">
<title> Computing the three spaces </title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following:
	<ol>
		<li> State the value of <m> k </m> so that the column space of <m> A </m> is a subspace of <m> \R^k </m>. </li>
		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-col-row-null-space-colspace-basis">
			Form a basis for the column space of <m> A </m> from the columns of <m> A </m>.
		</li>
		<li>
			Express each of the other columns of <m> A </m> as a linear combination of your basis vectors from
			<xref ref="instruction-exercisegroup-col-row-null-space-colspace-basis" text="custom">part</xref>.
		</li>
		<li> State the value of <m> k </m> so that the row space of <m> A </m> is a subspace of <m> \R^k </m>. </li>
		<li> Determine a basis for the row space of <m> A </m>.  </li>
		<li> State the value of <m> k </m> so that the null space of <m> A </m> is a subspace of <m> \R^k </m>. </li>
		<li> Determine a basis for the null space of <m> A </m>. </li>
		<li>
			State the rank and nullity of <m> A </m>,
			and confirm the <xref ref="theorem-col-row-null-space-rank-nullity" text="title" /> in this case.
		</li>
	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		A = \begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			 1 \amp 0 \amp  2 \\
			-2 \amp 1 \amp -4 \\
			 1 \amp 3 \amp  3
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p> First, note that in this case the RREF of <m> A </m> is the identity. </p>

		<p><ol>

			<li>
				There are <m> 3 </m> entries in each column,
				so the column space is a subspace of <m> \R^3 </m>.
			</li>

			<li> All three columns of <m> A </m> are required to form a basis for the column space. </li>

			<li> In this case, there are no <q> other </q> columns. </li>

			<li>
				There are <m> 3 </m> entries in each row,
				so the row space is a subspace of <m> \R^3 </m>.
			</li>

			<li> The standard basis of <m> \R^3 </m> is a basis for the row space of <m> A </m>. </li>

			<li>
				As the coefficient matrix of a homogenous system,
				there are <m> 3 </m> variable coefficients in each row (equation),
				so the null space is a subspace of <m> \R^3 </m>.
			</li>

			<li>
				In this case,
				the null space is the trivial space <m> \{ \zerovec \} </m>,
				with basis the empty set <m> \{ \} </m>.
			</li>

			<li>
				The rank is <m> 3 </m> and the nullity is <m> 0 </m>.
				These two numbers add up to <m> 3 </m>,
				and this sum agrees with the number of columns of <m> A </m>,
				confirming <xref ref="theorem-col-row-null-space-rank-nullity" text="title" />.
			</li>

		</ol></p>

	</answer>

</exercise>

\left(\begin{array}{rrr}
\end{array}\right)

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		A = \begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			 0 \amp  1 \amp  4 \\
			-1 \amp  1 \amp  1 \\
			-1 \amp -1 \amp -7
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer>
		<p>
			First, reduce <m> A </m> to RREF:
			<md>
				\RREF(A) = \begin{bmatrix}
					1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp 4 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp 0
				\end{bmatrix}

			</md>.
		</p>

		<p><ol>

			<li>
				There are <m> 3 </m> entries in each column,
				so the column space is a subspace of <m> \R^3 </m>.
			</li>

			<li>
				Use the first two columns:
				<md>
					\text{column space of } A =
					\Span \left\{
						\begin{abmatrix}{r} 0 \\ -1 \\ -1 \end{abmatrix},
						\begin{abmatrix}{r} 1 \\  1 \\ -1 \end{abmatrix}
					\right\}
				</md>.
			</li>

			<li><m>\displaystyle
				\begin{abmatrix}{r} 4 \\ 1 \\ -7 \end{abmatrix}
				= 3 \begin{abmatrix}{r} 0 \\ -1 \\ -1 \end{abmatrix}
				+ 4 \begin{abmatrix}{r} 1 \\  1 \\ -1 \end{abmatrix}
			</m></li>

			<li>
				There are <m> 3 </m> entries in each row,
				so the row space is a subspace of <m> \R^3 </m>.
			</li>

			<li>
				Use the nonzero rows of <m> \RREF(A) </m>:
				<md>
					\text{row space of } A =
					\Span \left\{
						\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix},
						\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
					\right\}
				</md>.
			</li>

			<li>
				As the coefficient matrix of a homogenous system,
				there are <m> 3 </m> variable coefficients in each row (equation),
				so the null space is a subspace of <m> \R^3 </m>.
			</li>

			<li><m>\displaystyle
				\text{null space of } A =
				\Span \left\{ \begin{abmatrix}{r} -3 \\ -4 \\ 1 \end{abmatrix} \right\}
			</m>.</li>

			<li>
				The rank is <m> 2 </m> and the nullity is <m> 1 </m>.
				These two numbers add up to <m> 3 </m>,
				and this sum agrees with the number of columns .of <m> A </m>,
				confirming <xref ref="theorem-col-row-null-space-rank-nullity" text="title" />.
			</li>

		</ol></p>

	</answer>

</exercise>


</exercisegroup>
